    scenes: [
      { t: "Module one: blockchain and coins. We will see how a shared record of transfers works, who decides which block comes next, how bitcoin is issued, and how smart contracts, tokens, stablecoins and second layers differ." },
      { t: "A bank keeps its record of money to itself. A blockchain does the same, but the record is kept by thousands of computers, and none of them is in charge. Transfers are gathered into blocks, and blocks are linked by hashes. A hash is a fingerprint of data: change one letter, and the whole fingerprint changes.", head: "Block, hash and chain" },
      { t: "In the lesson you can change one digit in a block and see how the hash changes. Each block stores the hash of the previous one. So forging an old record means recalculating every block after it, faster than the rest of the network adds new ones.", head: "Try it yourself" },
      { t: "Who adds the next block is decided by consensus. In proof of work, the right goes to whoever first finds the required number by brute force, and that costs hardware and electricity. In proof of stake, blocks are proposed by validators who put up a stake, and for cheating they lose part of it.", head: "Consensus: work or stake" },
      { t: "Both methods share one weak spot: the majority attack. Whoever controls more than half of the power, or a large part of the stake, can rewrite recent blocks and spend the same coins twice. In big networks this costs billions; in small ones such attacks have happened. That is why an exchange credits a transfer only after several confirmations.", head: "Majority attack", kicker: "WEAK SPOT", points: ["more than half of the power or stake — recent blocks can be rewritten", "in big networks this costs billions of dollars; in small ones it has happened", "so an exchange waits for several confirmations of a transfer"] },
      { t: "New bitcoins appear only as a block reward. Every 210,000 blocks, roughly every four years, the reward is halved. There will be just under 21 million coins in total, and more than 95 percent are already issued. But limited issuance does not force the price up: it is known in advance, and the price is moved by demand.", head: "Bitcoin issuance" },
      { t: "Ethereum stores not only transfers but also programs: smart contracts. A contract is like a vending machine: the rules are written in code and are the same for everyone. It cannot be secretly changed or stopped. But a bug in the code cannot be quickly fixed either: the network will execute it just as precisely.", head: "Smart contracts" },
      { t: "Anyone can issue a token in a few minutes, so a token by itself says nothing about value. Stablecoins try to be worth exactly one dollar. Money-backed ones rely on the issuing company, crypto-backed ones on collateral in a contract, algorithmic ones on a promise to swap for their own token. In May 2022 that is how UST collapsed, and tens of billions of dollars vanished with it.", head: "Three kinds of stablecoins" },
      { t: "The network's work is not free. In Ethereum it is measured in gas: a simple transfer is 21,000 units, a swap several times more. The fee is gas multiplied by the gas price. The base part is burned, the rest is a tip to the validator. When everyone wants to get into a block, the gas price rises.", head: "Gas" },
      { t: "Hence the idea of a second layer. A rollup gathers many transfers into a batch and writes the whole batch to the main network at once, and the fee is shared by everyone. Optimistic rollups treat a batch as valid until someone proves otherwise, so a withdrawal can take a week. ZK rollups attach a mathematical proof. The price of cheapness is new links of risk: the operator and the bridge.", head: "Second layer" },
      { t: "The main points. A blockchain is a shared record that cannot be quietly rewritten. Who adds a block is decided by work or stake. And network coins, tokens, stablecoins and second layers are different things, each with its own answer to the question of whom you trust. Details are in the lessons below this video." },
    ],
